<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Shapes on Our Plates – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Shapes on Our Plates</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's a Square Cracker.</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Listening: Receptive: Recognize familiar words and basic phrases.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Interactive: Identify objects in the immediate environment in response to simple questions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Listening: Can recognize simple sentences about food shapes (e.g., "Oranges are round.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can follow basic instructions involving food shapes (e.g., "Find the round apple.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can respond to questions about food shapes using words or gestures (e.g., "What shape is the cracker?" "A square.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key vocabulary and concepts related to food shapes.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher initiates a "Simon Says" TPR game using food shape flashcards and realia (e.g., a real apple, a cracker). Teacher says, "Simon says, 'Touch the round apple!'" or "Simon says, 'Show me the square cracker!'" Students listen and follow the instructions, pointing to the correct item or flashcard. This activity introduces new vocabulary (round, square, apple, cracker) through listening and physical response.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of an apple and a cracker. For the apple, the teacher says, "Listen! This is a *round* apple." The teacher traces the shape with their finger and exaggerates the /r/ sound in "round." For the cracker, the teacher says, "Listen! This is a *square* cracker." The teacher traces the shape and exaggerates the /k/ sound in "cracker." The teacher then points to different food flashcards (e.g., banana, lemon, lime, papaya, mango, pineapple) and names them, asking students to listen and repeat chorally: "banana," "lemon," "lime."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of shapes and food vocabulary.
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing an apple flashcard) "Is this square or round?" (Students point to "round" flashcard or say "Round!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a cracker flashcard) "Is this food?" (Students nod "Yes!" or say "Yes!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a banana flashcard) "What is this?" (Students say "Banana!" or point to "banana" flashcard).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Shapes on Our Plates."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple audio story (or teacher-read with expressive voice) accompanied by large, colorful picture flashcards of a round apple, a square cracker, an oval banana, and a round orange. Genre: Descriptive sentences. Register: Informal, child-friendly. Format: Short audio recording or teacher reading aloud with visual aids.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Action:</b> The teacher presents a short audio clip or reads aloud a simple text, pointing to corresponding flashcards as the words are heard. The text should integrate grammar (Adjective + noun, Present simple, WH-questions) and vocabulary (pineapple, lemon, lime, mango, papaya, cracker, banana, food, juice box, snack, meal, round, square, oval, big, small, healthy, tasty, orange, brown, white).
                    <br>Example script: "Listen: Hello, friends! Look at my plate! I have a *round* apple. (Points to apple). I have a *square* cracker. (Points to cracker). This *banana* is *oval*. (Points to banana). What shape is the *orange*? It's *round*! All healthy food! Let's eat!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence (Pragmatic):</b> Identifying food shapes: Identifying and describing foods by shape (e.g., "The cracker is square.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Listening focus):</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "Listen to the story again. What foods do you hear?" (Students listen and point to the flashcards of apple, cracker, banana, orange).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Visual Check):</b> Teacher makes statements, students listen and indicate true/false by nodding/shaking head or thumbs up/down while looking at the flashcards.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The cracker is round." (Students shake head).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The apple is round." (Students nod).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The banana is square." (Students shake head, or teacher prompts, "What shape is the banana?" and students point to oval).</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening comprehension and recognition of target vocabulary and grammar.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice listening for and recognizing:
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Adjective + noun ("round apple," "square cracker"), WH-questions ("What shape is it?"), Present simple ("Oranges are round.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> pineapple, lemon, lime, mango, papaya, cracker, banana, food, juice box, snack, meal, round, square, oval, big, small, healthy, tasty, orange.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Focusing on identifying sounds like /r/ in "round" and /k/ in "cracker," and phonemic awareness for /æ/ in "apple."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listen & Point):</b> The teacher places various food flashcards (apple, cracker, banana, lemon, mango, pineapple, juice box) and shape flashcards (round, square, oval) on the board or floor. The teacher says instructions like: "Point to the *round* apple." "Point to the *square* cracker." "Point to the *oval* banana." "Point to the *big* pineapple." "Point to the *small* lemon." Students listen carefully and point to the correct flashcard or realia. This reinforces adjective+noun agreement and specific vocabulary recognition.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Listen & Draw/Match):</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of different food items (e.g., an apple, a cracker, a banana, a juice box) and empty boxes next to them, or a simple matching exercise. The teacher gives instructions: "Draw a circle around the *round* food." "Draw a square around the *square* cracker." "Match the *oval* banana to the oval shape." "Circle the *orange* juice box." Students listen and follow the drawing or matching commands. This activity practices listening for specific adjectives and nouns to complete a visual task.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Listen & Respond (Gestures/Words):</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard (e.g., a round lemon). Teacher asks, "What shape is *this*?" Students listen and respond using a gesture (drawing a circle in the air) or a single word ("Round!"). Teacher repeats with other items: "What shape is the *cracker*?" (Students say "Square!"). "What shape is the *mango*?" (Students can point to an oval flashcard or say "Oval!"). This practices listening to WH-questions and responding with basic vocabulary.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Shapes on Our Plates."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Healthy Plate" - a guided, collaborative listening and building activity. Students will listen to the teacher's instructions to place different food shapes on their paper plates.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Each student is given a paper "plate" and a set of pre-cut paper food shapes (e.g., a round apple, a square cracker, an oval banana, a round orange, a triangle slice of papaya).
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives a series of instructions, one by one. Students listen and place the corresponding food shape on their paper plate. For example:
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Put the *round* apple on your plate." (Students listen and place the apple).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Now, put a *square* cracker on your plate." (Students listen and place the cracker).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Find the *oval* banana. Put it on your plate." (Students listen and place the banana).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is your plate *healthy*? Yes!" (Teacher asks, students nod).</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher then asks individual students (pointing to their plate): "What shape is *this*?" (pointing to the cracker). The student listens and responds with a single word or gesture ("Square!"). This stage ensures students are actively listening to combine shape adjectives and food nouns in context.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in listening comprehension and identifying shapes/foods, collaborative interaction with the teacher's instructions, and successful task completion (creating a plate of specific food shapes).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment to check students' listening comprehension of food shapes and vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher will observe students individually or in small groups during the assessment task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> "Listen and Follow Directions Game."
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher holds up a tray with 4-5 different food flashcards (e.g., apple, cracker, banana, lemon, pineapple).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives a series of 3 simple instructions. Students listen and perform the action, e.g., pointing to the correct item or holding it up.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Show me a *round* fruit." (Students point to the apple or lemon).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Find the *square* cracker." (Students point to the cracker).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a *big* pineapple or a *small* pineapple?" (Teacher holds up a flashcard, students respond "Big!" or "Small!").</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Listening focus):</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can recognize and follow simple instructions involving food shapes (e.g., "Show me the round apple.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify familiar food words and shapes (e.g., "round," "square," "apple," "cracker," "banana") when heard.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can respond to simple WH-questions about shape with a gesture or single word (e.g., "What shape is it?" - "Square!").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Listens actively and engages with tasks.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "Did you hear 'round' and 'square' words today?" (Students show thumbs up for Yes, thumbs down for No).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says: "Point to the food you liked to hear about." (Students point to a flashcard).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review of the lesson and planning for future learning based on student performance and teacher observations.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new food did you hear about today?" (Students point to a flashcard or say a word).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to listen to today (a shape or a food)?" (Students can point to a picture, or say "apple," "round," etc.).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening-focused lesson? (e.g., TPR, flashcards, explicit pronunciation modeling for /r/ and /k/, simple commands).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face in listening comprehension or vocabulary recognition? (e.g., confusing "lemon" and "lime," difficulty with multi-word instructions, differentiating between "big" and "small" when heard).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to support listening and build on this vocabulary? (e.g., more repetition, use of a song with shapes, clearer visual cues, pre-teaching specific tricky sounds).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Great listening today, everyone! Next time, we will *read* about these same yummy foods and their shapes in a book. You will see the words for 'round' and 'square' and 'apple'!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>